# libwebp 1.6.0, vendored at sys/src/external/libwebp.
#
# ONE ARCHIVE, where upstream builds five (libwebp, libwebpdecoder,
# libwebpdemux, libwebpmux, libsharpyuv).  Plan 9 has no shared
# libraries, an unused archive member is never linked in, and a single
# `#pragma lib' in `<webp/types.h>' -- which every public header
# includes -- then serves every consumer with no `LIB=' line anywhere.
# *Splitting them would buy nothing and cost four more pragmas to keep
# in step.*
#
# **THE FILE LIST IS UPSTREAM'S, CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.**  Every
# `.c' on disk in these seven directories appears in that directory's
# own `Makefile.am', and no `Makefile.am' names a `.c' that is not on
# disk -- so "all of them" and "upstream's list" are the same 125 files
# here.  The command is
#
#	comm -23 <(ls $d/*.c | xargs -n1 basename | sort -u) \
#	         <(grep -ohE '[a-z0-9_]+\.c' $d/Makefile.am | sort -u)
#
# and it is empty for all seven.  *Re-run it after an import*: a new
# upstream file that nobody adds here compiles for no one and the link
# fails far away.
#
# **The 55 SIMD files (53 in `dsp/', 2 in `sharpyuv/') are in the list
# ON PURPOSE and compile to nothing.**  Each is wrapped in `#if
# defined(WEBP_USE_SSE2)' (or SSE41, AVX2, NEON, MSA, MIPS32,
# MIPS_DSP_R2), and kencc defines none of those, so the object is
# empty -- which is upstream's own arrangement for a target with no
# SIMD, not a failure.  `VP8GetCPUInfo' is NULL to match; see
# `dsp/cpu.c' and the note in docs/notes/ports.md.
#
# ***THE OFILES LIST IS GENERATED FROM DISK, NOT TYPED***, and the
# first hand-written one proves why: it carried `yuv_msa.$O', which
# does not exist, and omitted `lossless_avx2.c' and
# `lossless_enc_avx2.c', which do.  The check is two commands and
# belongs in any round that touches this list:
#
#	sed -n '/^OFILES=/,/^$/p' mkfile | grep -oE '[a-z0-9_]+\.\$O'
#	ls $d/*.c | xargs -n1 basename
#
# sorted and `comm'd.  *A missing entry is a link error in another
# package and a surplus one is a build failure here*, and neither
# names this file.
#
# `-D_POSIX_SOURCE' is the whole flag set, measured: all 125 compile
# with nothing else, and `WEBP_USE_THREAD' is deliberately OFF, so
# `thread_utils.c' is the single-threaded stub.  *libap has pthreads
# now, so that is a choice and can be revisited* -- but a first port
# that is single-threaded is one fewer thing to attribute.

